      "math_academy_id": "q-158207",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-y}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,y\\ne {x}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne {x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the curve is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed curve $y={x}^{2}$ and shade *above* and *below* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14273376/assets/e6fff00937aa9a20c6e8c1e83dd65c483ae6b403b55b4ac3054d4032249ffedb.png)",
